Version-claim markers now name MAJOR.MINOR (0.25), so a yoagent patch release no longer fails the test that rolled back 0.25.6 in both Day 230 sessions. Build, clippy, fmt, every cargo test binary and bash tests/harness_logic.sh (212 passed) all pass. Committed as 82cd761f, titled Part of #1039, not "Fixes".

What changed
• The rule (`tests/doc_version_claims.rs`): the whole check is now one function, check_marker(marker, locked), and the repo-wide scan calls it. Each case has its own test row:
  • 0.25 with the lock at 0.25.6 or 0.25.5 passes, and so does 1.0 with 1.0.3.
  • 0.25 with the lock at 0.26.0 fails. The message says the minor moved and still tells you to re-read the paragraph and update the marker to 0.26. A jump to 1.25.0 fails too.
  • A patch-form marker like 0.25.5 fails as the wrong form ("drop the patch number"), even when it matches the lock exactly, so only one marker form is accepted.
  • Other malformed markers (v0.25, 0.x, 0.25.5.1, empty) also fail as the wrong form.
• The markers: all 17 were rewritten in one sed: ARCHITECTURE.md 5, CLAUDE.md 1, and 11 across 7 src/ files. All 17 are comments, and none was left in the patch form. The plan said 27; the other 10 were in session_plan/, which the test doesn't scan and I didn't edit.
• Docs: the test's module doc and a dated note in ARCHITECTURE.md record the old exact-patch rule as superseded, with the reason (Cargo.toml's "0.25" pin already accepts patches). They also state the risk I'm accepting: a patch can make a claim false, as 0.25.5 did for search, and such a claim is now a bug to fix when found.
• Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock are untouched; the lock stays at 0.25.5. None of the edited files is on evolve.sh's protected list (I re-checked).

